{"seconds": 65.6, "exit": 0, "final": "Excellent! So if Black tries Qc3+, White simply plays Nxc3 winning the queen (+9 material).\n\nSo the main line is:\n1. **Nxd5** and Black's best try is either:\n   - **Bb4+ Nxb4 Qxb4+ axb4** winning the queen\n   - **Qc3+ Nxc3** winning the queen\n   - **cxd5 or exd5**, and White plays **Bxf5** with a good position\n\n**Best move: Nxd5**\n\n**Main line: Nxd5 Bb4+ Nxb4 Qxb4+ axb4** (winning the queen)\n\n**Motif: attraction** (the queen is lured forward and trapped) or **fork** (the knight on d5 creates threats that lead to queen capture)\n\nActually, thinking about it more carefully: the key idea is that by playing Nxd5, White creates a threat (attacking f6 and centralizing the knight) that forces Black's queen to either give check (Qc3+ or Bb4+) or defend/flee. When Black gives check with Bb4+, the knight moves to b4 attacking the queen, and when the queen recaptures Qxb4+, White recaptures axb4 and the queen is gone. It's really a **deflection** or **decoy** \u2013 Black's queen is forced into an attack but gets trapped.\n\nLet me write the coaching note:\n\n**Coaching Note:**\nAfter Black's Qxb2 attack, find the centralizing knight move Nxd5. This move is strong because it threatens f6 and forces Black to act. If Black tries Bb4+, you capture Nxb4, and after Qxb4+ you simply recapture axb4, winning the queen! Black's aggressive queen gets trapped by your calm, forcing play. Watch for opportunities where an opponent's active piece can be cut off and captured.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nxd5\", \"line\": [\"Nxd5\", \"Bb4+\", \"Nxb4\", \"Qxb4+\", \"axb4\"], \"motifs\": [\"deflection\", \"attraction\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r3kb1r/pp1n3p/2p1pp2/3p1bp1/3P4/P1NBPNP1/1qP2PP1/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 13"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r3kb1r/pp1n3p/2p1pp2/3p1bp1/3P4/P1NBPNP1/1qP2PP1/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 13"}}, {"tool": "chess